n" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "14 – 15 August 1936"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/323"} "Sportforum, Turnhalle, Reichssportfeld, Berlin"] " / " [:a {:href "/venues/324"} "Haus des Sports, Kuppelsaal, Reichssportfeld, Berlin"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/82931"} "71 from 26 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Sabre had long been a traditional Hungarian speciality and they had produced the Olympic champion every time they had competed since " [:a {:href "/results/72859"} "1908"] ". Therefore, it came as no surprise when Hungarian fencers filled three of the top four positions at the Berlin Olympics.  The 1936 Olympic champion was " [:a {:href "/athletes/22470"} "Endre Kabos"] ", a member of the victorious " [:a {:href "/results/81603"} "Hungarian team in Los Angeles"] ", who had twice won the European Championships since then. Kabos was actually beaten in the final pool by silver medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/22684"} "Gustavo Marzi"] " of Italy, but the Italian lost his chance of gold by losing two other bouts in the closing stage of the competition. The bronze medal went to future Olympic champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/22451"} "Aladár Gerevich"] ", also of Hungary."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Kabos, who was Jewish, was killed in 1944 when an explosion from a pipe bomb that had been planted on the Margaret Bridge, in Budapest, by a Hungarian neo-fascist group, exploded near where he was driving a munitions truck."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "286"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/22470"} "Endre Kabos"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: midd